{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>The threshing-floor of Atad.</b> Does a thorn-bush have a threshing-floor? Indeed, the atad is one of the species of thorns; rather, the name of the place was called thus, just as we find (1 Chronicles 13): ״ויבאו עד גורן כידון״ — 'and they came as far as the threshing-floor of Kidon' — and surely kidon is one of the weapons of war; rather, it was called גורן כידון — the threshing-floor of Kidon — because it was surrounded by weapons of war, for thus David said to Goliath the Philistine (1 Samuel 17): ״אתה בא אלי בחרב ובחנית ובכידון״ — 'You come to me with sword and spear and javelin.'" "<b>And they mourned there a very great and heavy mourning.</b> There are nine kinds of mourning: misped, nehi, tza'akah, heyhel, nidah, marze'ach, kinot, hegeh, and haval. Misped, as is said (1 Kings 13): ״ויספדו עליו הוי אחי״. Nehi, as is said (Jeremiah 9): ״קול נהי נשמע מציון״. Tza'akah and heyhel, as is said (Ezekiel 21): ״זעק והילל בן אדם״. Nidah, as is said (Jeremiah 48): ״נודו לו כל סביביו״. Marze'ach, as is said (Jeremiah 16): ״כה אמר ה' אל תבא בית מרזח״. Kinot, hegeh, and haval, as is said (Ezekiel 2): ״וכתוב אליה קינים והגה והי״. And when Scripture mentions here 'a very great and heavy mourning,' it includes all nine kinds of mourning, and the verse teaches you that nine groups went up with Joseph and mourned over him with nine kinds of mourning." "<b>And behold</b> the eulogy is a recollection of the kindnesses of the deceased and his noble qualities, as in the case of Saul and Jonathan: ״הנאהבים והנעימים בחייהם ובמותם לא נפרדו״ — 'the beloved and pleasant in their lives, and in their death not divided.' And according to the pain of the mourning, as is said (2 Samuel 1): ״צר לי עליך אחי יהונתן״. And according to what befell him, as is said (2 Samuel 3): ״ויקונן המלך אל אבנר ויאמר הכמות נבל ימות אבנר״. And likewise a person must recount the good that he had and that departed from him, as in (Ezekiel 27): ״בן אדם שא על צור קינים ואמרת לצור היושבת על מבואות ים״, and it is written there: ״הונך ועזבוניך מערבך מלחיך וחובליך מחזיקי בדקך ועורבי מערבך וכל אנשי מלחמתך אשר בך ובכל קהלך אשר בתוכך יפלו בלב ימים ביום מפלתך״. And our Rabbis, of blessed memory, mentioned this as well, that the essence of the eulogy is to recount the kindnesses of the deceased, as in the case of that one concerning Rabbi Pedaṯ, who opened with: 'This eulogizer — today is hard for Israel like the day the sun was brought at noon,' as is said (Amos 8): ״והיה ביום ההוא נאם ה' והבאתי השמש בצהרים והחשכתי לארץ״. Thus Rabbenu Hananel, of blessed memory, wrote in his commentary, and he further wrote: 'We eulogize over the head of a court — the dust of his grave is myrrh and aloes; he expounds נגעים and אהלות; who removes uncertainties and elucidates scattered halakhot — Torah he gathered, students he assembled, like one donning a breastplate, in the city of Ja'betz' — end of quote. <b>But seven days.</b> This mourning was before the burial, as it is written afterwards: ״וישאו אתו בניו ויקברו אותו״ — 'and his sons carried him and buried him.' But the mourning we are commanded by the law of the Torah is after the burial, after the stone is sealed."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 50, :notes {2 "The prooftext for kinot, hegeh, and haval is cited as Ezekiel 2; the verse וכתוב אליה קינים והגה והי is Ezekiel 2:10. The opening word והנה in the lemma of comment 2 appears to be a scribal remnant or erroneous lemma tag; translated as it stands."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:22:09.710717308Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}